1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,357
Welcome to this Cytomove walkthrough.

2
00:00:03,357 --> 00:00:18,553
In this tutorial, we will move through the complete scratch assay workflow: image quality control, analysis, result review, and publication-ready figure export.

3
00:00:18,553 --> 00:00:28,892
The goal is to keep each decision visible, reproducible, and easy to audit before the data leaves the browser.

4
00:00:28,892 --> 00:00:32,857
The workflow begins in Image QC.

5
00:00:32,857 --> 00:00:38,267
Here, each image can be reviewed before analysis starts.

6
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:51,250
Cytomove lets you inspect the field of view, confirm the scratch orientation, and define a clean crop region around the biologically relevant area.

7
00:00:51,250 --> 00:01:02,142
These checks are important because small framing or orientation problems can influence downstream segmentation and wound measurements.

8
00:01:02,142 --> 00:01:07,678
As the crop box is adjusted, the preview updates immediately.

9
00:01:07,678 --> 00:01:16,714
The original image is preserved, while the reviewed QC state becomes the input for analysis.

10
00:01:16,714 --> 00:01:25,089
This makes the correction step reversible and keeps the analysis based on an explicit image review.

11
00:01:25,089 --> 00:01:31,785
After QC is confirmed, the workflow moves into Analysis.

12
00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:46,142
Cytomove detects the wound region and overlays contours on the microscopy image, so the segmentation can be reviewed directly against the cells and the scratch boundary.

13
00:01:46,142 --> 00:01:51,035
The control panel supports practical review of the mask.

14
00:01:51,035 --> 00:01:58,821
Threshold and contour settings can be adjusted while watching the overlay respond on the image.

15
00:01:58,821 --> 00:02:12,053
This is especially useful when the scratch edge is irregular, when debris is present in the wound area, or when local contrast varies across the field.

16
00:02:12,053 --> 00:02:16,839
The purpose is not to hide the segmentation step.

17
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:29,392
Instead, Cytomove keeps it visible, so the user can judge whether the contour follows the biologically meaningful wound boundary before accepting the measurement.

18
00:02:29,392 --> 00:02:36,750
Once the contour is accepted, Cytomove summarizes the quantitative outputs.

19
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:46,446
The interface reports wound area, wound width, and closure-related measurements for the selected image and group.

20
00:02:46,446 --> 00:02:54,964
This makes it possible to move from visual review to numerical interpretation without changing tools.

21
00:02:54,964 --> 00:03:04,321
Across time points and experimental groups, Cytomove organizes the measurements into plots and tables.

22
00:03:04,321 --> 00:03:15,732
The user can compare control and treatment behavior, inspect the time course, and check whether the measurements agree with the image-level evidence.

23
00:03:15,732 --> 00:03:20,892
The final part of the workflow is the Publication Figure Builder.

24
00:03:20,892 --> 00:03:30,017
Here, reviewed images, contours, group summaries, and plots can be assembled into a figure layout.

25
00:03:30,017 --> 00:03:40,589
The builder is designed for manuscript preparation, so the visual elements remain connected to the reviewed analysis outputs.

26
00:03:40,589 --> 00:03:50,107
The figure can include representative microscopy panels, quantitative charts, and a short interpretation panel.

27
00:03:50,107 --> 00:04:02,839
Export options are prepared for publication workflows, including high-resolution image output and bundled files for review or downstream editing.

28
00:04:02,839 --> 00:04:17,821
This completes the Cytomove workflow: review images first, run transparent analysis, inspect the measurements, and export figures from the same reproducible session.

